---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { site } from '../../site';

const catalog = `catalog.${site.domain}`;
---

<Docs
  title="Scrobbling"
  toc={[
    ['how', 'How it works'],
    ['hardcover', 'Hardcover'],
    ['open-library', 'Open Library reading log'],
    ['gpodder', 'gPodder sync'],
    ['privacy', 'Tokens and privacy'],
    ['self-hosting', 'Self-hosting'],
  ]}
  description={`Report what you listen to in ${site.name} to Hardcover, your Open Library reading log and gPodder-compatible servers.`}
>
  <h1>Scrobbling</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Add-ons can hear what you play and pass it on: your reading tracker learns which book you are in
    the middle of, and your other podcast apps continue where you stopped. Two such add-ons come
    with {site.name}’s catalog service: Hardcover and gPodder sync.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
  <p>
    While you listen, the app notes when you start, about every five minutes of playing, when you
    pause and when you finish. These notes wait on your device and go to every add-on you installed
    that asks for them. Offline listening is sent once you are online again, with the times you
    actually listened. A failing add-on is tried again later, for up to 30 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The notes say what you played (title, author, IDs such as ISBNs, or the podcast feed and
    episode), where you were and when. They never contain where the audio comes from or the paths of
    your own files. Add-ons follow the profile: hide one from a profile in its settings and that
    profile’s listening isn’t reported to it. The protocol side is in{' '}
    <a href="/docs/protocol/#scrobble">Protocol v1 → Scrobble</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hardcover">Hardcover</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://hardcover.app">Hardcover</a> tracks what you read. With its add-on, a book you
    start listening to moves to <strong>Currently reading</strong> with your progress, and moves to{' '}
    <strong>Read</strong> when you finish it. Your <strong>Want to read</strong> shelf shows on
    Home, and books on it that an add-on can play open as usual.
  </p>
  <p>
    The add-on can also show rows from Hardcover: the books trending this month and of all time,
    your own lists (private ones too), the lists you follow, Hardcover’s featured lists and any
    public list you add by its link. You choose which; each list is one row, in its own order, up to
    12.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      On Hardcover, open Settings → Hardcover API and make a token with the permissions
      <code>read:me</code>, <code>read:library</code>, <code>write:library</code>,{' '}
      <code>read:catalog</code> and <code>read:lists</code>. Tokens made before August 2026 have
      them all.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open <code>https://{catalog}/hardcover/configure</code>, paste the token, choose your rows and
      make your add-on link.
    </li>
    <li>In {site.name}, open Settings → Add-ons and paste the link.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    To change the rows later, open the add-on link with <code>configure</code> in place of{' '}
    <code>manifest.json</code>, and replace the add-on with the new link. Rows you add or rename on
    Hardcover show once you choose them again there.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rows are read with your token only, never with one of ours: Hardcover’s terms allow lists to
    be read only for the person they belong to or who chose to see them. Answers are kept for a few
    hours per token, under a hash of it, so your token’s daily limit lasts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Books are matched by ISBN or Audible ASIN when the add-on that lists them knows one, and
    otherwise by title and author. Books Hardcover doesn’t know are skipped. Hardcover tokens expire
    after a while; when yours does, make a new link the same way and replace the add-on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-library">Open Library reading log</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://openlibrary.org">Open Library</a> keeps a reading log for every account. With
    this add-on, a book you start listening to goes on <strong>Currently reading</strong>, and moves
    to <strong>Already read</strong> when you finish it. Only the book and the shelf are sent: no
    times, no progress, no ratings.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Open Library has no API tokens; apps sign in with your archive.org S3 keys instead of your
      password. Signed in with the account you use on Open Library, open{' '}
      <a href="https://archive.org/account/s3.php">archive.org/account/s3.php</a> and copy the
      access key and the secret key.
    </li>
    <li>
      In {site.name}, open Settings → Add-ons. Under Comes with {site.name}, choose Set up next to
      Open Library reading log, paste both keys and choose Check and save.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The add-on runs inside the app, in a sandbox that can reach only openlibrary.org. The keys stay
    on your phone, sealed with its key store, and are never synced, exported or sent anywhere else;
    no {site.name} server sees them. Books from the Catalog are Open Library works already. Books
    from other add-ons count when the add-on gives their Open Library work ID; all others, your own
    files among them, are skipped rather than guessed. The keys also work for the rest of
    archive.org, such as uploads. To stop the add-on, remove it, or make new keys on the same
    archive.org page.
  </p>
  <p>
    It works in the Android app only. Open Library signs apps in with a cookie, and a browser never
    shows a page the cookie, so the web app offers the add-on but can’t set it up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gpodder">gPodder sync</h2>
  <p>
    gPodder-compatible servers keep the places of podcast episodes for several apps at once, such as
    gpodder.net or a Nextcloud with the gPodder Sync app. With this add-on, every episode you play
    in {site.name} is reported as a play action with where you started and stopped.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Open <code>https://{catalog}/gpodder/configure</code> and choose the server type: the
      gpodder.net API, or Nextcloud gPodder Sync.
    </li>
    <li>
      Enter the server address (https only), your user name and your password. Use an app password
      where the server offers one. The add-on checks the login before it makes the link.
    </li>
    <li>In {site.name}, open Settings → Add-ons and paste the link.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The gPodder API identifies episodes by their audio URL, which the app never sends. The add-on
    reads the podcast’s public feed to look it up by the episode’s <code>guid</code>; only the
    feed’s text passes through it, never audio.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">Tokens and privacy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your token or password is sealed into the add-on link with a key only the catalog service has
      (AES-256-GCM). It isn’t stored on the server, and a link copied out of a log or a screenshot
      doesn’t show it.
    </li>
    <li>
      The link itself still works for whoever has it, so keep it to yourself. {site.name} keeps it
      on your device and syncs it end-to-end encrypted. To stop a leaked link, revoke the token or
      change the password at the service.
    </li>
    <li>
      The add-ons are stateless: they act on each batch of events and forget it. What you played is
      kept by Hardcover or your gPodder server, under their terms.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hardcover’s rows are kept in the server’s cache for a few hours, under a hash of your token
      that can’t be turned back into it. Only a request with the same token gets them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="self-hosting">Self-hosting</h2>
  <p>
    Both add-ons are part of the catalog service, on Workers and in the <code>catalog</code> Docker
    image. Set <code>CONFIG_SECRET</code> to at least 32 random characters (on Workers with{' '}
    <code>wrangler secret put CONFIG_SECRET</code>); without it, the configure pages say they are
    unavailable. Keep it stable: changing it makes every link stop working until it is configured
    again. On Docker, the gPodder add-on can’t reach private addresses unless you set{' '}
    <code>SCROBBLE_ALLOW_PRIVATE=true</code>, for a gPodder server on your own network. See{' '}
    <a href="/docs/hosting/">Hosting</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
